59,3,,status,George,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
61,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option B is the most logical for reducing physical friction. By removing fixed ownership, we can reorganize the layout to stop the shoving matches and ensure no one is permanently stuck in the rain.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option A is out of the question. As I mentioned, the treasurer is already overburdened, and adding monthly billing for individual spaces would be an administrative nightmare. We need a system that requires zero financial tracking.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,George,,"Option C addresses the stagnation I found in the minutes. A lottery ensures that the best spots aren't held by the same people forever, providing the transparency and fairness the residents are lacking.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Option A is a disaster waiting to happen. As I noted, fees turn neighbors into strangers. We don't need to turn our lobby into a cold zone just to balance a ledger.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Mio,,I'm with Ryuji and Shunichi on this! Option A sounds like a total headache for the treasurer. Let's not turn our home into a business office with all that monthly billing!,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@George, while a lottery is fair on paper, it doesn't solve the physical chaos. If the layout remains the same, we'll still have people shifting bikes and dealing with rain, just with different names on the spots.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, you're right. Option B solves the physical layout issues and requires no financial tracking. Option C still leaves us with fixed spots that might be poorly positioned, and it requires an annual administrative process.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,George,,"@Ryuji, an annual draw is a small price to pay for fairness. Option B might seem simple, but without assigned spots, the chaos of people fighting for the best remaining space every single day could be worse.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, you're trading long-term stagnation for daily combat. A lottery just gives people a formal reason to resent whoever won the ""dry"" spot this year. Option B is far less likely to create those lasting grudges.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@George, a lottery sounds like a lot of work to organize every year! Who's going to handle the applications and the draw? I'm worried it'll just be another job that someone forgets to do after a while.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"Option B is the only choice that addresses the actual behavior in the parking area. If we remove the concept of ""my spot,"" we can implement a layout that prioritizes flow and accessibility, ending the morning shoving matches.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,Option B is the most rational choice. It eliminates the administrative burden of billing or annual draws and solves the physical issues Asuka identified. It is the only option that is truly sustainable for the treasurer.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,George,,"I understand the desire for simplicity, but Option B ignores the reality of human nature. Without assigned spots, the ""best"" spaces will still be claimed by the fastest or most aggressive residents, creating a different kind of unfairness.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, you're worrying about the ""aggressive"" few while ignoring the risk of turning the whole building into a battleground. I'd rather have a bit of daily shuffle than a formal system that breeds long-term resentment.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@George, I see your point, but I'm just not okay with adding more chores to our list! Option B is so much easier. No billing, no draws, just park and go! It's the most cheerful way to do it!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Asuka,,Option B is the only solution that prioritizes the physical reality of the space. It removes the source of conflict and the administrative burden. I vote for Option B.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,Option B is the only choice that aligns with both the financial constraints and the need for administrative simplicity. It avoids the pitfalls of Option A and C entirely. I vote for Option B.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,George,,"I cannot ignore the fact that the current system has been stagnant for five years. While Option B is simple, it doesn't guarantee fairness for those who aren't the fastest. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"George, your obsession with ""fairness"" is exactly how you end up with a building full of people who avoid eye contact. Option B is the only one that doesn't turn our living situation into a bureaucratic exercise. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Mio,,I'm totally with the majority! Option B is the only one that doesn't create a mountain of paperwork or a yearly chore for someone to manage. Let's keep it simple and stress-free! I vote for Option B!,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
